TypeScript is a statically-typed superset of JavaScript that adds optional type annotations to the language. It was developed by Microsoft and released in 2012. TypeScript is designed to build large-scale applications with better tooling, improved code completion, and stronger type safety, while still being fully compatible with JavaScript.

Duck Typing, on the other hand, is a concept that emphasizes the behavior of objects rather than their declared types. It is a way of performing runtime type checking in dynamically-typed languages like JavaScript, without the use of class hierarchies or interfaces.

In Duck Typing, an object is said to be of a certain type if it exhibits the same behavior as another object that is known to be of that type. This is often referred to as "duck checking" or "duck testing." The idea is similar to the expression "if it walks like a duck and quacks like a duck, then it must be a duck."

TypeScript and Duck Typing are not mutually exclusive. TypeScript provides a way to define types for variables, functions, and classes, but it doesn't enforce them at runtime. In TypeScript, you can still perform Duck Typing by checking the behavior of objects, even if you define types.

For example, consider the following TypeScript code:

```typescript
interface Bird {
  fly(): void;
  quack(): void;
}

function makeSound(animal: Bird): void {
  animal.quack();
}

class Duck {
  fly(): void {
    console.log("Duck is flying");
  }
  quack(): void {
    console.log("Quack!");
  }
}

makeSound(new Duck()); // Quack!
```

In this example, we define an interface `Bird` with two methods `fly()` and `quack()`. We then create a function `makeSound` that expects an argument of type `Bird`. We also define a class `Duck` that implements the `quack()` method of the `Bird` interface.

When we call `makeSound` with a new instance of `Duck`, TypeScript knows that the type of the argument passed to `makeSound` is compatible with the `Bird` interface, even though we don't define `Duck` as implementing the `fly()` method. This is because TypeScript uses structural typing, which means that types are based on their shape (i.e., the set of properties and methods they have) rather than their names.

In this sense, TypeScript allows for Duck Typing by allowing structural compatibility between types, while also providing optional type annotations and interfaces to increase type safety and expressiveness.

To create a Discord bot with the `ban` command as a slash command using Discord.js v14, follow these steps:

1. Create a new Discord application on the [Discord Developer Portal](https://discord.com/developers/applications).

2. Add a new Bot to the application and copy its token.

3. Install Discord.js and its dependencies:

   ```
   npm install discord.js @discordjs/rest @discordjs/opus
   ```

4. Create a new `.env` file at the root of your project directory and add the following line:

   ```
   TOKEN=<your-bot-token>
   ```

   Replace `<your-bot-token>` with the token you copied in step 2.

5. In the same directory, create a new file called `index.js` and add the following code:

   ```javascript
   const { Client, GatewayIntentBits } = require('discord.js');
   const { REST } = require('@discordjs/rest');

   const client = new Client({ intents: [GatewayIntentBits.Guilds] });
   const rest = new REST().setToken(process.env.TOKEN);

   // Define your commands
   const commands = [
     {
       name: 'ban',
       description: 'Bans a user from the current guild.',
       execute: (interaction) => {
         // Add your command logic here
         // For example:
         const member = interaction.options.getMember('user');
         if (!member) {
           return interaction.reply({ content: 'No user found to ban.', ephemeral: true });
         }
         member.ban({ reason: `Banned by ${interaction.user.username}` });
         interaction.reply({ content: `${member.user.username} has been banned.` });
       },
     },
   ];

   async function registerCommands() {
     try {
       const commandsData = commands.map((command) => {
         return {
           name: command.name,
           description: command.description,
           type: 1,
         };
       });

       await rest.put(Routes.applicationCommands(process.env.APPLICATION_ID), { body: commandsData });
     } catch (error) {
       console.error('Error registering commands:', error);
     }
   }

   async function start() {
     try {
       await client.login(process.env.TOKEN);
       console.log('Bot is online.');

       await registerCommands();
     } catch (error) {
       console.error('Error logging in bot:', error);
     }
   }

   start();
   ```

6. Replace `<your-bot-token>` and `<your-application-id>` with the actual values from your Discord Developer Portal.

7. Save the file and run it:

   ```
   node index.js
   ```

8. In the Discord Developer Portal, add the bot to your server and grant it the `Ban Members` permission.

9. In your Discord server, type `/ban` in a channel to test your command.

That's it! Your Discord bot now has a working `ban` slash command. Customize the command logic as needed.

